WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:25.066
This moment, this exact feeling.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:39.998
I wish I could save it for another time.

00:00:44.961 --> 00:00:47.213
According to Walter Benjamin

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:50.675
Art before mechanical reproduction, i.e.

00:00:50.675 --> 00:00:52.719
photography had an aura.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.513
Aura is the here
and now of the work of art.

00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:58.850
It's this unique existence
and nothing else that bears

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.270
the mark of the history
to which the work has been subject.

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.565
Aura is basically the stuff
that the original has,

00:01:05.565 --> 00:01:09.319
but the reproduction,
no matter how precise it is,

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:13.406
it doesn't have because the original
is tied to a specific time and place.

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:14.866
But now that we have the capability

00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:17.869
to reproduce things,
we don't really have to restrict ourselves

00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:20.622
to going to the louver
to see the Mona Lisa.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:22.165
And according to Benjamin,

00:01:22.165 --> 00:01:25.794
this is a very important distinction,
and it changes our relationship to art.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:29.589
He goes as far as to say
that the more you replicate

00:01:29.589 --> 00:01:32.550
a piece of art,
the value of that art goes down.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:35.220
And it's not just art objects.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:36.971
Some places also have an aura.

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.016
I'm sitting on this floor

00:01:40.016 --> 00:01:43.853
listening to the seashore,
and I feel the aura of the beach.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:45.939
But when I take this home
so that I can have

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.151
a little piece of the beach
all the time, it has less aura

00:01:50.693 --> 00:01:53.530
and this has even less aura.

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:58.034
Aura is attached to the thing
and its purely unmediated form.

00:01:58.576 --> 00:02:02.205
The beach is there, sharing the same space
and time as me.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.625
Today
you see lots of mediations of the beach,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:09.045
and that shows the change
in our societal values.

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:14.300
We value things for their ability
to be seen by the largest number of people

00:02:14.592 --> 00:02:17.470
rather than for their unique existence.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:22.392
And apparently we're accelerating
this loss of aura with our constant

00:02:22.392 --> 00:02:26.104
desire to pry an object from its shell

00:02:26.604 --> 00:02:30.275
or to be close to something
or to be able to examine it.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.611
But mind you, Walter
Benjamin wrote this theory in 1936,

00:02:33.862 --> 00:02:38.116
and maybe if he was alive today, he
would have had a totally different idea,

00:02:38.116 --> 00:02:39.742
like what a about and of TS.

00:02:39.742 --> 00:02:42.912
It could be the one
and only copy in this world,

00:02:42.954 --> 00:02:45.206
but you know, it
lives in the digital realm.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.919
So the concept of its presence in space
and time is kind

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:51.462
of totally irrelevant, right?

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:53.381
But I think the

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:57.135
part of this theory that still is very
relevant

00:02:57.135 --> 00:03:01.181
is how we interact with objects
that we deem to have an aura

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:04.392
when an object is deemed
to have an intrinsic value.

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:09.564
We might guarded or hide it away
and we probably spend

00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:14.027
more time looking at it
in a more contemplative manner.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:18.239
When people say when you go to look
at the Mona Lisa, try to experi

00:03:18.239 --> 00:03:21.159
in the painting
and try to be in its presence.

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:25.663
But with reproducible works of art,
we tend to look at it

00:03:25.663 --> 00:03:30.460
less carefully or speed through it
in a less focused manner.

00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.337
It's the same with information.

00:03:32.337 --> 00:03:35.882
When books were handwritten over time
span of years,

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.135
they used to hide them from the public
because they were scared

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:42.555
that they were going to get damaged
or burned or lost, and they would

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:45.266
literally change the books to the desks.

00:03:45.767 --> 00:03:49.562
And what happened is,
when you finally got a hold of the book,

00:03:49.812 --> 00:03:53.608
you would read it
and really taken every word of the book.

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:57.320
But now, with massive amounts
of information everywhere we go.

00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:02.951
I think it's pretty safe to say that we
take things a little less to heart. Hmm.

00:04:03.493 --> 00:04:09.040
Umberto Eco, author and Semiotician, said
that books in his library had an aura.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:12.669
He had a collection of 50,000 books,
and he said as he would roam

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:18.007
around his library on more than one
occasion, he would stumble across a book.

00:04:18.383 --> 00:04:22.345
And when he read it, that book
magically contained information

00:04:22.345 --> 00:04:24.013
that was vital to the research.

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:27.517
He was a really strange person,
but he called his library and

00:04:27.517 --> 00:04:31.020
and he library and he filled it with books
that he'd never read.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.107
He said, lots of people have a library
to show

00:04:34.107 --> 00:04:37.777
off their knowledge, to see
look at all these books I read.

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:42.031
But he uses it as a research tool
and he roams around his library.

00:04:42.282 --> 00:04:45.535
And I think he believes that he is

00:04:45.827 --> 00:04:49.664
physically inches away
from discovering new information.

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:53.543
And that physical presence of the books

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:57.338
had the power to pull him
closer to what he needed.

00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:02.969
This idea of aura or the loss of aura in
our culture isn't just happening with art

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.972
or information,
but it's also happening with

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:14.814
our built environments.

00:05:18.693 --> 00:05:19.986
Cities are made

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:24.282
up of streets, and streets
are characterized by the buildings.

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:25.033
Facades.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:28.578
A building's
facade is basically a fancy name.

00:05:28.786 --> 00:05:32.832
Well, for the building's face,
it expresses the building's character,

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:35.793
and it gives you a glimpse
of what might be happening inside.

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.921
And it's designed to catch people's
attention.

00:05:39.255 --> 00:05:45.345
With our culture's obsession with images,
we experience architecture differently.

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:49.682
And our environments are adapting
to satisfy this new way of experiencing

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:53.561
architecture all the way down
to the process of how we design.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:57.857
We tend to focus more
on the shareable moments in our buildings,

00:05:58.149 --> 00:06:00.777
and I am totally guilty of this.

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:05.239
Architecture is becoming
increasingly rushed and impatient,

00:06:05.656 --> 00:06:08.785
leading to issues
of experiential impoverishment.

00:06:08.826 --> 00:06:12.622
Our built environments
are void of any sense of mystery or aura.

00:06:12.914 --> 00:06:15.875
And just like what's happened
with art or information,

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:21.339
I think the way we experience the world
is becoming more and more one dimensional

00:06:21.839 --> 00:06:25.968
and we start to value architecture,
not necessarily

00:06:25.968 --> 00:06:30.890
for its experiential qualities,
but for its ability to be photographed

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:39.732
and seen by the largest number of people.

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:42.735
Venice is

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:47.323
probably one of the most extreme
examples of a city

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:51.702
whose aura has been completely
and utterly destroyed.

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:54.956
Thanks to the iconic images,
it's become one

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:58.334
of the most popular
tourist destinations in the world.

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:02.171
It gets something around
20 million visitors every year.

00:07:02.547 --> 00:07:05.758
It shows movies and books,
and it's probably

00:07:05.758 --> 00:07:08.761
one of the most universally
recognizable locations.

00:07:08.803 --> 00:07:09.679
I'm not going to lie.

00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:13.933
When I first visited Venice,
I also had my preconceptions until

00:07:14.183 --> 00:07:16.644
I learned this concept called the Reef,

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:19.730
which directly translates into drifting.

00:07:19.897 --> 00:07:24.444
It's actually a method devised
by the Situationist International

00:07:24.444 --> 00:07:29.949
for navigating through Venice,
and they define the reef as an unplanned

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:33.035
and unexpected passage
through an urban landscape

00:07:33.035 --> 00:07:37.373
where participants stop their daily
routines and allow themselves to be drawn

00:07:37.373 --> 00:07:41.502
by the attractions of the terrain
and the encounters that they find there.

00:07:41.794 --> 00:07:44.922
It's a place of both connection
and non connection.

00:07:45.256 --> 00:07:48.509
A place designed
specifically for getting lost

00:07:48.801 --> 00:07:51.179
from a totally functional point of view.

00:07:51.429 --> 00:07:53.556
Venice's kind of the worst place to live.

00:07:54.182 --> 00:07:56.642
It floats. It's full of tourists.

00:07:56.893 --> 00:07:58.478
The buildings are old.

00:07:58.478 --> 00:08:02.857
It's practically not accessible
for anyone with mobility issues.

00:08:03.065 --> 00:08:05.109
And you can't drive. You can't bike.

00:08:05.318 --> 00:08:09.822
And in that sense, it's
the ultimate antiquity.

00:08:09.906 --> 00:08:14.076
So why is it that so many Venetians
who's been driven

00:08:14.076 --> 00:08:18.706
out of the city for all of these reasons
that I just mentioned, still dream

00:08:18.706 --> 00:08:22.418
about one day
moving back there and living there.

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:41.145
I think it ultimately
has something to do with oral,

00:08:41.145 --> 00:08:44.857
but in the end it's a totally
and completely unique place.

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:47.902
There's no other place like it on Earth.

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:51.364
There's no one else
that can replicate the environment,

00:08:51.405 --> 00:08:55.826
the history,
and the city's presence in time and space.

00:08:56.035 --> 00:09:00.331
Not even the most perfect
replica has the aura of Venice.

00:09:00.414 --> 00:09:04.627
But as an architect, I still try to look
for patterns in the built environment

00:09:05.044 --> 00:09:08.756
not necessarily to replicate,
but to see if there's any elements

00:09:08.756 --> 00:09:09.757
that we can learn from.

00:09:09.757 --> 00:09:11.259
And as I tried to study the built

00:09:11.259 --> 00:09:15.096
fabric to understand why
it feels so different from other cities,

00:09:15.096 --> 00:09:19.934
I was able to distill certain elements
or recurring conditions

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:22.645
that helped in
creating the sense of mystery.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.397
Framing.

00:09:24.605 --> 00:09:27.608
Framing is used to hide
or highlight certain elements.

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:32.321
In this case, a narrow gap between
the corners of two buildings perfectly

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:35.866
frame
the corner of the Basilica de Ferrari

00:09:36.117 --> 00:09:39.870
deadens a key aspect of the drive.

00:09:39.912 --> 00:09:44.750
Or drifting is allowing yourself to be
drawn by the attractions of the terrain.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:49.463
And as I drift through these alleys,
I kept coming across these dead ends.

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:53.634
Initially, I'd turn around and backtrack,
but on instances

00:09:53.634 --> 00:09:57.096
when I kept going, I found that
it's not a dead end after all.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.891
And the corner reveals
another alley point of interest

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:03.477
as I move through these narrow alleys.

00:10:03.519 --> 00:10:08.107
I kept feeling a constant
pulling like a push to keep walking.

00:10:08.816 --> 00:10:09.567
It's obviously

00:10:09.567 --> 00:10:13.779
the narrowness of the tunnel,
but also the light at the end of the alley

00:10:13.779 --> 00:10:18.075
or a tunnel that created this point
of interest, compression and release.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.245
When I follow the light
and reach the end of the tunnel,

00:10:21.412 --> 00:10:26.334
I would often be pleasantly surprised
to find myself in a light filled courtyard

00:10:26.626 --> 00:10:31.213
or an opening to a canal or anything,
but created a feeling of expansion.

00:10:31.547 --> 00:10:35.593
And as I walk for hours and hours,
I felt a constant

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:38.721
compression and release in the landscape.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:44.560
Layering, layering creates anticipation,
depth, and a more interesting

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.023
and memorable narrative
when walking through space.

00:10:49.565 --> 00:10:54.779
Well, all of these conditions do, for me
is it created a sense of curiosity

00:10:54.779 --> 00:10:59.200
that made me want to discover more
and also a kind of safety.

00:10:59.283 --> 00:11:03.704
I never once felt scared
or like I was in danger.

00:11:03.704 --> 00:11:07.750
The city is so, so safe
because there's no dangerous corners.

00:11:07.792 --> 00:11:10.795
That sounds like
a really obvious statement, but let's say

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:14.423
a typical city
with streets and back alleys, the alleys

00:11:14.423 --> 00:11:18.260
or the leftover spaces and you know,
that's where all the dodgy stuff happens.

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:22.890
But in Venice,
everything is an alley and there's no

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:27.103
front and back and everything
is just a continuous labyrinth.

00:11:27.103 --> 00:11:29.897
And there's doors
and windows onto each path.

00:11:29.939 --> 00:11:34.068
So after walking around for a while
and just being left by my curiosity,

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:36.696
that's when I discovered really cool gems.

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:39.448
Lots of people
think Venice is a static city.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:43.285
I mean, there's been virtually
no new construction in Venice

00:11:43.285 --> 00:11:47.915
for hundreds of years, but actually
it has been transforming at a micro-scale

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.753
since we can only guess the functions
inside the buildings urge us to discover

00:11:52.753 --> 00:11:55.715
more rather than telling us
exactly what they are.

00:11:55.965 --> 00:11:59.135
You see this especially in
the neighborhood of Cannery Row,

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:04.682
which was once a Jewish ghetto, these
seemingly benign residential buildings.

00:12:04.682 --> 00:12:09.770
When you go inside, they have synagogues
with ornate, lavish details

00:12:10.062 --> 00:12:15.401
that you can only discover
by exploring further inside.

00:12:17.445 --> 00:12:19.905
It's our knowledge that things that we're
sure of

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:23.826
that makes the world go wrong
and keeps us from seeing and learning.

00:12:23.909 --> 00:12:27.121
We have a tendency to put the things
that we know

00:12:27.121 --> 00:12:31.542
or knowledge on a pedestal
and treat it as the ideal truth.

00:12:31.917 --> 00:12:36.839
When our knowledge is often incomplete
and subjective.

00:12:37.006 --> 00:12:41.260
Venice only started to reveal itself to me
when I left

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:46.724
my preconceived ideas of Venice
and kind of allowed myself to be lost

00:12:46.724 --> 00:12:50.895
and blindly led by my curiosities
and instincts.

00:12:51.103 --> 00:12:54.982
I let my feet choose the wrong turns and

00:12:55.274 --> 00:12:59.320
and I let the light or the textures
draw me into different directions.

00:12:59.653 --> 00:13:02.865
And only then did
I discover a side of Venice

00:13:02.865 --> 00:13:06.786
that was totally
and intimately unique to me.

00:13:07.077 --> 00:13:09.163
This Venice is fleeting.

00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:14.585
You can't photograph it, and you can't
take it with you, and it can't be shared.

00:13:14.835 --> 00:13:19.256
This Venice can only be experienced
and live as a memory.

00:13:19.757 --> 00:13:23.135
This Venice has an aura
that can't be destroyed

00:13:23.844 --> 00:13:26.931
because it can never be replicated
and never fully

00:13:26.931 --> 00:13:47.201
attained.

